ty requires Congress to establish statutory requirements for independent, tiered auditing before any frontier model reaches the public. Effective regulatory regimes separate the entity building the product from the entity testing the product. Under the current paradigm, the AI Safety Institute lacks the enforcement teeth to compel access, leaving regulators dependent on voluntary corporate cooperation. Congress must draft legislation that funds and empowers independent auditing boards—staffed by civil rights experts and data scientists—to legally compel model access and halt deployments that fail bias benchmarks. This argument assumes that independent auditors can secure sufficient compute power and technical access to meaningfully stress-test frontier models; if they cannot, we may be forced to rely entirely on strict post-deployment liability frameworks to punish harm after the fact. Ultimately, until we shift the power of evaluation from the developers to independent public advocates, AI safety will remain a corporate public relations strategy rather than a democratic safeguard.",
