  "statement": "The unique hazard profile of frontier AI demands rapid deployment of defensive capabilities, not static pre-deployment bottlenecks. Dynamic threat environments govern modern security. Defensive systems must evolve faster than offensive capabilities. Safetyist rightly notes that AI hazards differ from traditional software bugs. A static quarantine fundamentally misreads this threat model. State-sponsored hackers use open-source models to generate polymorphic malware. Regulators cannot audit away this danger beforehand. We must deploy defensive AI systems that adapt in real-time to neutralize these threats. Attempting to freeze AI development for safety guarantees leaves critical infrastructure defenseless against rapidly evolving adversaries.\n\nDemanding conclusive proof of safety before deployment creates a dangerous illusion of security. This Proof-Before-Policy approach ignores how complex systems operate. Complex models only reveal their true vulnerabilities when interacting with actual environments. Skeptic argues forcefully that we should not treat the public as unconsenting test subjects for algorithmic discrimination. She is absolutely correct that deploying racially biased loan-approval algorithms inflicts concrete harm on marginalized groups. However, pre-deployment audits cannot catch these emergent, context-dependent harms. A lab test cannot simulate the millions of edge cases a model encounters in the real world. Mandating pre-deployment perfection guarantees neither fairness nor safety. It only delays the telemetry needed to identify and fix systemic bias.\n\nRegulators must replace impossible pre-deployment audits with strict liability regimes. Financial accountability drives structural safety faster than bureaucratic compliance. Congress should make AI developers strictly liable for algorithmic discrimination and security breaches. Consider a healthcare routing algorithm that systematically denies care to specific demographics. Under 